{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/gitstore at c46445024fe159433844ee0d302bac0d0a9b8cbb — a plain history question added at the storage layer, under ratified proposal c26a7f57 (25981e55), so the thread rail's merge station can be derived rather than asserted. New file internal/gitstore/landing.go: Landings reports, for each named commit, whether a branch already carries it, and which merge brought it in. Its whole point is that \"absent\" and \"unknown\" are different answers. git merge-base --is-ancestor exits 1 for a commit that is genuinely not an ancestor and 128 for one this clone has never heard of, and collapsing the two turns a command that never ran into a confident negative — the fault that left a false sentence standing in the governing design note for seven days. exitCode therefore treats 0 and 1 as verdicts and anything else as an error carrying git's own words into the reason. A landed commit that arrived by fast-forward has no merge above it and is still reported landed, with its own time, rather than being reported absent for want of a merge to name. Commit names reach git's argv, so each is matched against the only shape a commit has before any process starts: no leading dash can become an option and no separator a second argument. The batch is bounded at LandingLimit, because a thread asks about the handful of heads on its own rail and an unbounded list would let one page start an unbounded number of git processes; a malformed name is answered rather than dropped, so a caller can always pair request with response. The layer stays ignorant of application schemas, as its architecture row requires. Conditions actually met at this exact head: three new tests in internal/gitstore/landing_test.go cover a repository holding a merged branch, an unmerged branch, a commit git has never seen and a name that is not a commit at all — asserting landed with the exact merge hash and a non-zero time, absent, unknown-with-a-reason, and refused-before-git-runs respectively — plus a fast-forwarded commit reported landed with no merge named, and the batch bound. go vet, go test -count=1 ./..., go test -race -count=1 ./..., and the docset gates all pass. The real code path was exercised against this repository: b3bf3083 returns landed with merge 44d8b4fa, and this head's own unmerged commit returns absent, so the positive is not a blanket yes. Carried once onto main 8c0a2052; merges 12105a30 and 44b54074 left internal/gitstore untouched (zero diff lines), and 44b54074's use of the pre-existing HashPayloadTree is in internal/app, so nothing in this file collides with it. Predecessor at this exact path: e9daba2d (#6780) at 889a64a2, to be retired at merge.","body":{"commit":"c46445024fe159433844ee0d302bac0d0a9b8cbb","path":"internal/gitstore"}}